\begin{theorem}
Let $J$ be a prime Jordan algebra satisfying $f=0$. Suppose $[x, y]^2 = 0$ for all $x, y \in J$. Then $J \simeq J(v, f)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The difference between prime, nondegenerate, and primitive algebras: the class of prime and nondegenerate Jordan algebras is much larger than the class of primitive algebras. Primitivity imposes additional conditions related to the existence of specific types of inner ideals. Over a field of characteristic $\neq 2$, primitive Jordan algebras satisfying a polynomial identity must be simple and unital (e.g., finite-dimensional over their centers or Jordan algebras of a nondegenerate quadratic form).
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}
Inner (or quadratic) ideals serve as the counterpart to one-sided ideals in associative algebras. Unlike standard ideals, which are closed under multiplication by any element of the algebra, inner ideals are closed under the sandwich operation: for $b \in J$, the set $U_b(J) = \{ U_b(x) \mid x \in J \}$ is an inner ideal.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
The McCrimmon radical is locally nilpotent. In particular, a simple degenerate algebra is locally nilpotent.
\end{remark}
